# Purpose of the Project
The primary purpose of this project is to build an interactive, modular command-line interface (CLI)
application in Python that executes full CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations and keyword
searching over property listings. It emphasizes persistent file-based data handling via JSON serialization,
defensive programming with structured exception handling (try-except), and modular software design.



# Problem Statement
Real estate brokers and independent agents frequently face operational bottlenecks when relying on
manual or semi-automated spreadsheets:
• Enforcing unique identification across properties to prevent duplicate inventory records.
• Handling runtime crashes when users enter alphabetic text into numeric fields such as price, bedroom
counts, or square footage.
• Modifying specific property attributes without having to re-enter all other existing values from scratch.
• Retaining state across application restarts without relying on complex, heavy external database servers.
• Quickly filtering through large volumes of listings based on specific locations or property titles.


# Functional Requirements
The application consists of five operational business modules managed through a centralized 6-option CLI
menu loop.
Module 1: Data Persistence & Connection (connect.py)
Checks file existence via os.path.exists(), serializes Python objects to JSON with indentation
formatting, and deserializes JSON strings safely handling IOError and json.JSONDecodeError

Module 2: Property Registration (add.py)
Validates data types via try-except ValueError, verifies ID uniqueness against properties.json using
any(), packages fields into a dictionary, and appends it to storage.

Module 3: Catalog Viewing (view.py)
Fetches all records via connect_db(); formats price as currency (${price:,.2f}) and formats
dimensions.

Module 4: Selective Record Updating (update.py)
Searches property by id1; prompts for each attribute allowing empty inputs ("") to keep existing
values; converts numeric inputs safely with ValueError guards; writes changes back to disk.


Module 5: Deletion & Keyword Search (delete.py, search.py)
Delete: uses list-comprehension filtering [p for p in properties if p["id1"] != prop_id]; saves the
updated array if the record was found.

Search: normalizes the keyword to lowercase; matches the query substring against title.lower() and
location.lower().


# Non-Functional Requirements
• Data Persistence: Records remain persistent across sessions by serializing data into an external
properties.json document.
• Fault Tolerance & Error Handling: Prevents script termination on illegal user entries by wrapping input
type-casting (int(), float()) in structured try-except ValueError blocks.
• Maintainability & Modularity: Adheres to separation of concerns by isolating presentation (main.py),
storage management (connect.py), and individual CRUD routines across separate scripts.
• Usability: CLI prompts guide the user explicitly, allowing attributes to be preserved during updates by
pressing Enter.
